{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before dawn, when the world feels paused between forgetting and remembering. The streets are empty, the air is cool, and even the birds seem to be holding their breath. In that quiet, my thoughts become clearer, as if the noise of the day has finally loosened its grip. I think about small things: a letter never sent, a song heard once in a taxi, the taste of oranges in winter. None of them matter much alone, yet together they make a kind of map of who I have been. Sometimes I wonder if memory is less like a photograph and more like a tide, returning familiar shells in unfamiliar arrangements. We tell ourselves stories to keep the water from feeling too deep. Yet there is comfort in not knowing everything, in leaving some rooms of the past gently unopened. By the time sunlight touches the rooftops, people begin again with coffee, keys, and hurried goodbyes. I like witnessing that beginning, because it reminds me that renewal rarely announces itself with trumpets. More often, it arrives as ordinary light on an ordinary wall, asking only that we notice. If I could keep anything from these mornings, it would not be answers, but attention. Attention turns waiting into listening, strangers into neighbors, and brief encounters into something that quietly changes us. And when night returns, I hope I remember that darkness was never empty, only full of unseen beginnings waiting for morning to reveal them.",
